title: Money and the Market
card-image: img/cards/money.svg?v=2
subtitle: The ruble, the market and the peasants.
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: soviet_affairs
view-if: money_timer <= 0 and bol_regime = 1 and dix >= 30
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; money_timer = 3

[? if dix < 48 and nep_early = 0 : The Commissariat of Food holds the grain monopoly, and since May its squads have enforced it with rifles. The ruble, old notes and new alike, is printed by the trainload and is worth less than the paper. ?][? if dix >= 48 and dix < 72 and nep_early = 0 : Since January the requisition fixes a quota for every province before the harvest is in. Wages are paid in rations, and the peasants sell at the Sukharevka market or not at all. ?][? if dix >= 72 and dix < 96 and nep_early = 0 : Wages are paid in rations; the trams, the post and the rent cost nothing, and in the commissariats there is talk of abolishing money altogether. The Moscow Soviet speaks of closing the Sukharevka market, as if trade could be closed by decree. ?][? if dix >= 96 and dix < 100 and nep_early = 0 : The requisitions have stripped the villages bare, and the risings in Tambov and Siberia are the villages' answer. The Communist Party's congress meets in March, and everyone in Moscow expects something to change. ?][? if dix < 100 and nep_early = 1 : The tax in kind has come early, and the peasants may sell their surplus again. The ruble has not yet followed the grain to market. ?][? if dix >= 100 : The New Economic Policy has brought back the market, but not yet a currency anyone trusts. ?] The party can still say, and sometimes do, something about it. (The ruble: [+ ruble : ruble +].)

- @market: Campaign for a free grain market in place of requisitioning.
- @coops: Rebuild the cooperatives, which still trade with the villages.
- @currency: Call for a stable currency, backed by gold.
- @easy_discard

@market
view-if: dix < 100

How should the party go about it? Cautiously, with compromises, it will cost a resource, do less and anger less. In full, it will do all that it says, and its opponents will answer. And a party strong enough can carry it out and break its opponents.

- @market_slow: Go about it cautiously, with compromises.
- @market_decree: Carry it out in full.
- @market_force: Carry it out in full, and break any opposition by force.

@market_slow
choose-if: resources >= 1
subtitle: Milder effects and little anger. Costs 1 resource.
unavailable-subtitle: We have nothing to pay the compensation with.
on-arrival: {!

RO.law(Q, 'slow', () => {
RO.add(Q, {ruble: 4, bread: 2, rel_bol: -4, repression: 1});
RO.boost(Q, {peasants: 3, workers: 1});
}, "Campaign for a free grain market in place of requisitioning");
!}

The party's resolutions for a free grain market were read out at every congress that would hear them. In the provinces where the local authorities listened, grain came to market and the ruble was worth something again. In Moscow, the Cheka took note. (It was done cautiously, with compromises.)



@market_decree
subtitle: All that it says. Its opponents will answer.
on-arrival: {!

RO.law(Q, 'decree', () => {
RO.add(Q, {ruble: 4, bread: 2, rel_bol: -4, repression: 1});
RO.boost(Q, {peasants: 3, workers: 1});
}, "Campaign for a free grain market in place of requisitioning");
!}

The party's resolutions for a free grain market were read out at every congress that would hear them. In the provinces where the local authorities listened, grain came to market and the ruble was worth something again. In Moscow, the Cheka took note.



@market_force
view-if: bol_regime = 0
choose-if: strength >= 18
subtitle: Your chance of breaking each camp it angers: Bolsheviks [+ odds_bol +]%. Any camp under 45% is too strong for you, and is left alone.
unavailable-subtitle: We lack the force and the allies to break the opposition.
on-arrival: {!

RO.law(Q, 'force', () => {
RO.add(Q, {ruble: 4, bread: 2, rel_bol: -4, repression: 1});
RO.boost(Q, {peasants: 3, workers: 1});
}, "Campaign for a free grain market in place of requisitioning");
!}

The party's resolutions for a free grain market were read out at every congress that would hear them. In the provinces where the local authorities listened, grain came to market and the ruble was worth something again. In Moscow, the Cheka took note. [+ law_text +]

@coops
on-arrival: {!
RO.add(Q, {ruble: 2, bread: 3});
RO.boost(Q, {peasants: 2, middle: 1});
RO.fac(Q, 'unions', 2, -2);
!}

[? if dix < 52 : The cooperatives were the last trading network that the state had not taken over. The party's members went to work in them, and some goods began to move between the towns and the villages. ?][? if dix >= 52 and dix < 100 : Since the spring of 1919 the cooperatives have been consumers' communes under the Commissariat of Food. The party's members work in them anyway, and some goods still move between the towns and the villages. ?][? if dix >= 100 : Under the New Economic Policy the cooperatives may trade on their own account again. The party's members, who kept them alive through the war, went back to running them, and goods began to move between the towns and the villages. ?]

@currency
choose-if: nep_early = 1 or dix >= 100
unavailable-subtitle: Nobody in power will listen while War Communism lasts.
on-arrival: {!
RO.add(Q, {ruble: 7});
RO.boost(Q, {middle: 2, workers: 1});
!}

The party's economists argued for a currency backed by gold, and some of them were asked to draft it. The chervonets that followed was not ours, but the argument was.
